    "id": "skool-vault-these-look-like-real-photos-theyre-not",
    "section": "vault",
    "platform": "skool",
    "status": "Idea",
    "title": "These look like real photos. They’re not.",
    "post": "No camera. No model. No studio. No photoshoot. Just AI.\n\nI created both images with GPT Image 2.0.\n\nAnd the biggest lesson? Photorealism isn't about adding more detail. It's about removing the things that make an image feel AI-generated.\n\nThink:\n\n→ imperfect skin instead of flawless skin\n→ believable lighting instead of dramatic lighting\n→ ordinary environments instead of perfect sets\n→ natural framing instead of studio composition\n→ tiny imperfections instead of polished perfection\n\nHere are the prompts I used.\n\n## IMAGE 01 — GARAGE SNAPSHOT\n\nCreate a highly realistic vertical smartphone photograph with the visual characteristics of an ordinary iPhone snapshot.\n\nShow an athletic man around 30 years old standing inside a lived-in residential garage during late afternoon. He has a close buzz cut, short facial stubble, a naturally muscular build, and is shirtless. He is slightly turned away from the camera and looking down while casually holding a small metal object between both hands.\n\nHis appearance should feel completely ordinary and believable rather than like a fitness model. Include natural body proportions, subtle skin variation, visible pores, tiny blemishes, fine body hair, slight perspiration and realistic skin color variation. He wears a simple thin gold necklace.\n\nThe garage should feel genuinely used: shelves containing miscellaneous household items, cardboard storage boxes, basic tools, an old refrigerator, dusty surfaces and random objects. Everything in the background should be generic and slightly out of focus. No identifiable products or brands.\n\nLighting should come naturally through an open garage entrance, producing soft directional daylight and realistic shadows. Avoid cinematic studio lighting.\n\nThe composition should feel accidental and handheld, as though someone quickly captured the moment on their phone. Slight framing imperfections, realistic lens behavior, subtle exposure variation and very fine digital camera grain.\n\nAvoid: perfect skin, excessive sharpness, artificial HDR, beauty retouching, CGI appearance, exaggerated muscles, dramatic lighting, symmetrical composition, text, logos, recognizable brands, watermarks, distorted anatomy, malformed hands or extra fingers.\n\nFormat: 9:16 vertical, high resolution.\n\n## IMAGE 02 — CASUAL MIRROR SELFIE\n\nGenerate an extremely believable vertical smartphone mirror photograph that could realistically appear in someone's personal camera roll.\n\nA woman in her late 20s is sitting casually on the edge of a bathroom counter, photographing herself through a large mirror. Show her from a natural three-quarter side angle. She has long dark hair gathered into a simple low ponytail, subtle everyday makeup and a relaxed, self-assured expression.\n\nShe is wearing a fitted cream-colored textured knit two-piece outfit with a modest, elegant appearance. Add understated gold earrings, a delicate bracelet and a simple ring. She holds a completely generic dark smartphone that hides part of her face. No recognizable phone design or branding.\n\nThe bathroom should look like a real expensive-but-lived-in home bathroom: pale stone surfaces, large mirror, glass shower enclosure, slightly uneven reflections, warm ceiling lighting and a few subtle signs of normal use. Keep the environment believable rather than excessively luxurious or staged.\n\nHer skin must retain natural human characteristics: visible pores, tiny imperfections, realistic texture, subtle redness, fine facial details and natural tonal variation. Makeup should look like actual makeup rather than an AI beauty filter.\n\nUse realistic smartphone exposure and lens characteristics. Slightly imperfect framing, natural depth of field and subtle digital grain. Nothing should look professionally photographed.\n\nAvoid: porcelain skin, excessive facial symmetry, beauty-filter appearance, plastic texture, exaggerated body proportions, artificial bokeh, CGI, illustration, excessive sharpening, text, logos, watermarks, distorted hands, extra fingers or recognizable real people.\n\nFormat: 9:16 vertical, high resolution.\n\nThe trick isn't simply saying \"make it photorealistic.\"\n\nGive the model the details that real photographs naturally contain: texture + imperfections + ordinary environments + believable lighting + imperfect framing.\n\nThat's where the realism starts.\n\nTool: GPT Image 2.0\nRatio: 9:16\nQuality: 4K / highest available\n\nThen change the person, clothes, location and lighting to build your own version.",
    "note": "Selected from AI Video Club for strong engagement and a useful comment discussion.",
